---

import { orgPaths } from '../../../lib/tenant-paths';
export const getStaticPaths = orgPaths;

import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../../../components/PageHead.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Terms of use"
  description="What SciPath is and is not, and what a record kept here does and does not prove."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Policies"
    title="Terms of use"
    lede="What the software is for, what a record kept in it is, and who remains in charge."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What SciPath is</h2>
      <p>
        SciPath is a planning and recordkeeping aid for student research. It keeps a
        project's calendar, its notebook, the documents written for a class or a
        fair, the feedback given on them, and the grades and outcomes recorded
        against them. It is not itself an authority on any of those things.
      </p>

      <h2>Who remains authoritative</h2>
      <p>
        Your adult sponsor, your teacher, your school's review committee (SRC or
        IRB where one applies), and the destination fair remain authoritative.
        Nothing SciPath records, prints, or displays substitutes for their
        approval, and a deadline, rubric, or rule shown here is a copy of theirs
        and yields to theirs where the two differ.
      </p>

      <h2>What the notebook is</h2>
      <p>
        Notebook entries are recorded append only: each one is attributed, carries
        the day the work happened, and is never edited afterwards. A correction
        appears as a later entry naming the one it corrects. This is a record kept
        in order, which is a discipline device rather than proof against a
        determined author, and it is offered as no more than that.
      </p>

      <h2>What a printed copy is</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF or printed copy of a notebook or a document is the record as it
        stood at the date and time printed on it. Later entries, later versions,
        and later feedback are not on it.
      </p>

      <h2>Your account and your work</h2>
      <p>
        You keep the copyright in what you write here. A school that runs SciPath
        may read the work its students record in the programs it runs, and the
        people it names as Elders, officers, teachers, or sponsors see what their
        role gives them. The privacy policy lists what is stored and how to have
        it deleted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
